2. SECURE GOOGLE LOGIN AND EMAIL AUTHENTICATION
==================================================

Fix the existing Google authentication implementation.

Requirements:
- Users must be able to authenticate using their own Google accounts.
- When Google login is selected, allow the user to select or authenticate with their own account.
- Do not automatically authenticate ordinary users using the owner's Google identity.
- Do not silently reuse the owner's account or grant the owner's permissions to another person.
- If the browser already has an active Google session, provide an appropriate account-selection or account-switching mechanism where necessary.
- Do not rely exclusively on the Google account currently active in the browser.
- Support secure sign-in using the application's existing email authentication method, if available.
- Properly handle cancelled login attempts, invalid credentials, expired sessions, and authentication failures.
- Do not expose access tokens, refresh tokens, passwords, private credentials, or other authentication secrets in the frontend.

IMPORTANT:
Google authentication identifies a user. It must not independently determine whether that user is an administrator.

