7. LIVE ADMIN LOGIN AND SESSION TRACKING
==================================================

Build a functional administrator dashboard for live authentication and session monitoring.

The dashboard must display real authentication and session information, not hardcoded, mocked, or randomly generated data.

A. Live session overview:
Display:
- Total registered users.
- Total currently active authenticated users.
- Total active guest sessions.
- Total currently active administrators.
- Total currently active owner sessions, visible only to appropriately authorized administrators.
- Recent login events.
- Active sessions across multiple devices.
- Last activity time where available.
- Successful and failed login events, subject to appropriate security and privacy controls.

B. Per-user login information:
For each authorized user record, display:
- Display name.
- Verified account email, where available and permitted.
- Account type: Owner, Administrator, Normal User, or Guest.
- Authentication method: Google, email, or guest.
- Login timestamp.
- Last activity timestamp.
- Current session status: Online, Offline, or Session Expired, as appropriate.
- Number of active sessions.
- Device category, such as desktop, tablet, or mobile, when available.
- Browser information when available and appropriate.
- Session identifier or a safe, non-secret session reference for distinguishing sessions.

Do not display passwords, access tokens, refresh tokens, or other sensitive authentication credentials.

C. Live tracking:
- Update session information automatically using a suitable mechanism, such as WebSockets, server-sent events, or controlled polling.
- Show new login events without requiring the administrator to refresh the entire page.
- Reflect logout events, expired sessions, and session revocations.
- Detect activity using server-observed session events and an appropriate heartbeat or activity mechanism.
- Distinguish between an active authenticated session and a user who is demonstrably active at that moment.
- Handle disconnected devices, browser closures, network failures, and delayed heartbeat messages.
- Use reasonable expiration windows so that a disconnected user is not incorrectly shown as permanently online.
- Support simultaneous sessions for the same user.
- Show separate entries for each device session where appropriate.

D. Track every supported application entry route:
The monitoring system must record authentication and session events whether the user accesses the application through:
- The shared application link.
- The deployed application's normal URL.
- A Google Cloud-hosted application URL.
- Another supported entry point that leads to the same authenticated application.

Do not assume that opening the app through Google Cloud automatically means that the person is the owner or an administrator.

If multiple URLs point to the same application, ensure that authentication and monitoring work consistently across those entry points.

Important limitation:
Opening a URL and successfully authenticating are different events. Record page visits separately from successful logins where technically supported. Do not label an unauthenticated visitor as an authenticated user.

E. Admin dashboard security:
- Only authorized administrators may access permitted user-monitoring information.
- Only the owner or an appropriately authorized administrator may access privileged administrative functions.
- Enforce these restrictions on the backend.
- Do not expose the complete user list or login-event data to normal users or guests.
- Audit access to sensitive monitoring information.

